> Box 2. The development-resilience link: Identifying private household resilience to guide ‘easy win’ entry points for public investment in climate resilience: Kenya case study
> Beyond risk reduction, the IPCC frames “climate-resilient development” as the joint pursuit of adaptation and risk mitigation to advance sustainable development for all, highlighting that resilience policies enhance human well-being, equity, and long-run growth when embedded in core development planning (e.g., infrastructure, urban systems, livelihoods). In other words, building resilience is not a parallel agenda—it is a pathway to achieve development outcomes more reliably under a changing climate. (IPCC, 2022; Schipper et al., 2022).
> This report shares preliminary findings of a forthcoming paper drawing on World Bank household surveys (2019–2022) to assess climate hazard impacts and coping mechanisms (Baeza-Breinbauer, forthcoming). While the goal of the forthcoming paper that this case study is based on is to specifically increase the more scarce pool of evidence regarding Indian Ocean Dipole (IOD) driven climate shocks with panel household data in Kenya, the goal of this report more largely is to guide investment priorities for greater resilience to shocks in general. Global evidence shows that climate shocks push households into poverty and keep them there by destroying assets, disrupting services and markets, and eroding human capital; hence, disaster risk management is “inseparable” from poverty reduction. (Hallegatte et al., 2017; IPCC, 2022).
> Investment requirements to boost resilience
> The Treasury highlights that the Financing Locally Led Climate Action (FLOCA) programme, implemented across 78% of Kenya’s wards, has created over 57,000 jobs and supported more than 500 sub-projects. FLOCA demonstrates how local ownership and vertical coordination between national and county governments can enhance adaptation outcomes and accountability. Resilient infrastructure (irrigation, flood-safe rural roads, reliable electricity) raises farm and non-farm productivity by improving market access and reducing downtime; Lifelines stresses that what matters for development is reliable services, not just assets, because service interruptions impose large output and welfare losses on firms and households. Hence, resilience upgrades crowd in private investment by lowering risk and transaction costs (Hallegatte et al., 2019).
> To reduce fiscal burden and strengthen resilience, investments must address hazard, vulnerability, and response determinants of climate shocks. The 2019 pIOD exposed Kenya’s agricultural sector to multiple hazards. Floods damaged infrastructure, disrupted markets, and reduced yields, while locusts harmed crops and grazing lands, worsened by pesticide shortages and unsafe control practices. Preliminary regression results from a forthcoming paper show exposure is shaped by livelihood, crop choice, and infrastructure. Potato and cassava growers are more flood-prone while banana and kale growers are less exposed to locusts. Electricity access is linked to higher locust exposure—perhaps driven by greater capacity for higher yields that attract locusts (Baeza Breinbauer, forthcoming).
> Vulnerability is in part driven by local government budgets, preparedness, wealth, and income diversity. Wealth enables post-shock recovery and diversification of income sources to smooth shocks, while weak government budgets limit safety net capacity. Social safety nets help but are often underfunded. Pre-existing poverty forces households to buy food when harvests fail. Preliminary regression results demonstrate that farm size is the strongest predictor of earnings, followed by household size and housing quality. Access to fertilizer and credit boosts incomes, but reliance on informal loans often signals distress (Baeza Breinbauer, forthcoming). The Treasury underscores that strengthening county-level fiscal capacity is critical to improving preparedness and reducing reliance on central government bailouts.
> With regards to determinants of vulnerability among agricultural workers, preliminary regression results highlight the synergistic nature of development and resilience. Findings indicate that farmers benefit most from access to land, fertilizer, and formal finance, while pastoralist incomes depend on credit and government assistance (Baeza Breinbauer, forthcoming). Wealth indicators improve earnings for both groups. Building resilience requires crop- and livelihood-specific interventions, better credit and input access, stronger safety nets, and sustainable budget frameworks. Because shocks induce poverty traps through asset loss and costly coping, investments that protect assets and services, alongside targeted social protection, accelerate income diversification and structural transformation—objectives core to both development and resilience goals (Hallegatte et al., 2017; IPCC, 2022).
> Burden on public budget of complex pIOD risk
> Climate shocks generate macro-fiscal risks—diverting budgets from growth-enhancing investments (health, education, roads) to emergency response—thereby slowing development. The World Bank’s Lifelines finds that resilient infrastructure and services protect firms’ and households’ productivity, with estimated net benefits of US$4.2 trillion in LMICs (≈ US$4 return per US$1 invested) by avoiding service disruptions that depress output and welfare. (Hallegatte et al., 2019).
> Given vulnerable groups already facing undernutrition, disease, and food price volatility are often hit hardest by climate shocks (Serdeczny et al., 2017), Kenya’s public programmes have been vital in mitigating impacts. However, this analysis finds that domestic expenditure in the form of public government aid programmes rose more than forty-fold after the 2019 IOD driven climate shocks, creating major fiscal pressures (Baeza Breinbauer, forthcoming). According to the National Treasury, scaling up social protection programmes has been vital for buffering vulnerable households against climate shocks, though this also risks fiscal pressures.
> Spending increases were driven by expanded coverage of social protection schemes rather than larger transfers: between 2019-2022, average benefits per household stayed between 2,100–3,400 Ksh, while coverage rose from <1% to 11% before dipping to 9%. Government financial support per 100 households peaked at nearly 28,000 Ksh before contracting, reflecting both welfare gains and fiscal risks, including crowding out of health, education, and infrastructure while risking increased public debt. Treasury officials note that while coverage expanded significantly, the government is working towards institutionalising climate-related aid spending through budget tagging and tracking systems to improve efficiency, transparency, and accountability.
> Evaluations of Kenya’s Hunger Safety Net Programme (HSNP) show that predictable cash transfers and emergency scale-ups during climate shocks stabilise consumption, reduce negative coping, and protect minimal assets—effects that preserve human capital and productive potential, especially when shocks hit. While most resources meet immediate needs (less for long-term investment among the very poorest), the development payoff comes from avoiding irreversible losses (e.g., distress sales, school dropout) (Merttens et al., 2017).
> Added benefits of investments
> Climate shocks damage infrastructure, disrupt markets, and strain resources, leaving communities with few options: rebuilding or shifting livelihoods if household budget or borrowing options allow, or land abandonment. Abandoning land often leads to permanent migration and uncultivated land, worsening desertification and flood risk. Desert Locust outbreaks add further risks, forcing trade-offs between crop destruction and hazardous pesticide spraying. Preliminary regression results demonstrate that floods are a consistent driver of hunger across agricultural workers, farmers, and pastoralists. Hunger is also linked to owning less assets (e.g. livestock), lower access to seeds or fertilizer, and reliance on reactive coping mechanisms (forthcoming, Baeza Breinbauer).
> Investing in climate resilience for Kenyan agricultural households provides benefits beyond fiscal savings, including avoided losses and co-benefits in the form of food security and welfare. Results show that better housing, electricity, inputs, and food expenditures lower hunger risks. Expanding adaptive credit, and safety nets reduces reliance on loans and child labour while stabilizing welfare. The National Treasury stresses that fiscal decision-making increasingly considers cost-effectiveness and long-term savings from disaster risk reduction. 
