> Box 5. The role of institutions in delivering adaptation outcomes: Rwanda Case Study
> Institutional and governance arrangements are central to delivering effective adaptation outcomes, particularly at the macroeconomic and fiscal levels. Governments play a critical role in resolving market failures, creating enabling conditions, and mobilising finance to deliver adaptation at scale. Adaptation is not a one-off action but an evolving process that requires iterative decision-making in response to changing risks. Effective institutional design, clear strategies, and adaptive governance mechanisms are essential. However, market failures and coordination challenges can hinder progress, underscoring the need for deliberate government intervention to establish the necessary policy, regulatory, and financial frameworks for adaptation.
> Institutional and enabling actions should be recognised as a key form of adaptation in their own right. They are foundational to achieving and sustaining adaptation outcomes, yet their importance at the macroeconomic and fiscal level has often been underexplored. This case study focuses on Rwanda, a country that has developed one of the most advanced and coherent climate policy frameworks globally, to examine how institutional arrangements and enabling conditions can enhance macroeconomic and fiscal adaptation.
> A literature review undertaken for this study identified three central themes underpinning successful macroeconomic and fiscal adaptation: 1) Institutional arrangements and coordination; 2) Strategies and mainstreaming; and 3) Enabling factors. Together, these elements mobilise finance, strengthen implementation effectiveness, and help scale up adaptation actions, although measuring these benefits quantitatively remains challenging. Building on these themes, a detailed institutional review, supported by social network mapping, and interviews with key government and institutional representatives further provided insights into the factors driving success.
> 
> The social mapping exercise visualises relationships between actors, responsibilities, and enabling mechanisms, resulting in the network diagram above, which illustrates the dense and interconnected institutional landscape that supports adaptation in Rwanda. It maps the roles of key organisations—such as the Ministry of Environment (MoE), the Ministry of Economic Planning and Finance (MINECOFIN), the Rwanda Environment Management Authority (REMA), and the Green Fund —alongside a range of ministries, local governments, financial institutions, and international partners. The colour-coded relationships highlight how responsibilities, mainstreaming mechanisms, and enabling factors are distributed and coordinated across the system.
> The institutional mapping and interviews revealed that strong coordination and distributed mandates across government have been critical in driving adaptation. While the Ministry of Environment plays a leading role, responsibilities extend across multiple ministries, including the Ministry of Finance and Economic Planning, ensuring that climate change considerations are embedded throughout the government apparatus.
> A particularly influential factor has been the integration—or mainstreaming—of adaptation into national development planning. MINCOFIN has incorporated climate change into Rwanda’s five-year development plans (RoR, 2017: RoR, 2024a), ensuring that adaptation priorities are not treated as stand-alone initiatives but as integral components of economic growth and fiscal stability. This mainstreaming has extended into medium-term expenditure frameworks, budgeting systems, and macroeconomic and fiscal analysis, effectively institutionalising climate risk considerations across public financial management systems. Most recently, MINECOFIN has developed and published a Climate and Nature Finance Strategy (CNFS) (RoR, 2024b), and is in the process of setting-up a designated climate finance department within the Ministry, to support climate resilient and low carbon development and investments at scale. 
> The mapping also found that Rwanda has implemented nearly all of the enabling factors identified in the academic and policy literature. These include the establishment of a country platform, a green taxonomy, programmatic adaptation investment planning, and mechanisms for risk disclosure and analysis. Among these, the Rwanda Green Fund (formerly FONERWA) stands out as a particularly impactful innovation. Functioning as both a country coordination platform and a financing mechanism, the Green Fund has successfully mobilised significant volumes of adaptation finance—especially concessional resources—while also catalysing private sector participation. Innovative instruments such as private sector climate facilities, on-lending schemes, blended finance mechanisms, equity investments, and green bonds have further diversified Rwanda’s adaptation finance landscape  The Green Fund started as a public sector orientated fund, targeting international public finance, and this continues as NDC Facility (Intego) (Intego, 2025). However, over time it has evolved to target wider resource mobilisation, and it has now added a blended facility model, the Rwanda Green Investment Facility (Ireme Invest) (Ireme, 2025), to develop new financial instruments to support and de-risk private sector investment. Because of these initiatives – and broader progress on institutional and governance – Rwanda has been extremely successful in mobilising international public finance for adaptation, with climate finance flows over the last five years tracked at US$840 million (UNEP, 2024),
> The analysis also highlighted innovative institutional arrangements within the financial sector. The Development Bank of Rwanda and the National Bank of Rwanda have played central roles in greening financial systems through climate-linked investment standards, climate stress testing, and participation in global initiatives such as the Network for Greening the Financial System (NGFS). These efforts have deepened the integration of climate risk into financial decision-making, reinforcing the systemic resilience of Rwanda’s economy.
> When interviewees were asked what had catalysed this high level of institutionalisation and coordination, a consistent theme emerged: strong political commitment and national recognition of climate risk. Climate change is widely acknowledged across the Rwandan government as a core development and fiscal concern, informed both by studies quantifying the costs of inaction and by recent climate shocks, notably the devastating 2023 landslides and their macroeconomic and fiscal repercussions. Capacity-building efforts have further strengthened institutional capabilities, reinforcing Rwanda’s leadership in integrating climate adaptation into fiscal and macroeconomic planning.
> Rwanda’s experience demonstrates how robust institutional frameworks, clear strategic direction, and comprehensive enabling mechanisms can transform adaptation from a policy ambition into an integrated component of national economic management.
> ===
> Box 6. Long-term risks require long-term planning: Bangladesh Case Study
>  
> Bangladesh has been identified as one of the countries with the greatest risks from climate change, with a vulnerable population exposed to multiple extreme hazards, including heat waves, rising sea-levels, and frequent flooding. To take one hazard, under climate change, Bangladesh faces higher rainfall in its wet months, potentially quadrupling the risk of extreme monsoon driving flooding by mid-century (Fahad et al. 2023, Kamruzzaman et al. 2024). These risks are already manifesting, with the worst floods in more than 100 years occurring in 2022. Studies have highlighted the disproportionate impact on women and the elderly, with potential for food insecurity and health risks (Hazrana et al. 2025). The long-term impacts of past disasters, like the 1970 cyclone, demonstrate lasting health, education, and economic adversities (Eskander & Barbier 2022).
> 
> Like many developing countries, Bangladesh aims to integrate the multiple demands of climate adaptation, economic development, and climate mitigation. Climate resilient development pathways (CRDP) has emerged as a central paradigm for doing this. CRDP recognises the role of forward-looking planning of actions when engaging in adaptation to risks that will continue evolving for decades.
>  
> Bangladesh has developed a state-of-the-art example of CRDP in the Bangladesh Delta Plan 2100 (BDP 2100). BDP 2100 is a comprehensive, multi-hazard and multi-sector plan, with the mission to “ensure long-term water and food security, economic growth, and environmental sustainability while effectively reducing vulnerability to natural disasters and building resilience to climate change and other delta challenges through robust, adaptive, and integrated strategies, and equitable water governance” (Arfanuzzaman 2024). If fully implemented, BDP 2100 is expected to eliminate a 5.6% decline in GDP that Bangladesh would otherwise experience (World Bank 2021).
> 
> More broadly, Bangladesh is notable in its approach to integrating climate adaptation and economic development. It has centred the national-level planning processes itself, producing significant documents such as the National Action Plan for Clean Cooking (2019), Sustainable Finance Policy (2020), National Strategy on Internal Displacement Management (2021), National Adaptation Plan (2022), Sector Action Plan on Environment and Climate Change (2023), and Mujib Climate Prosperity Plan (2024).
>  
> Together, the BDP 2100, National Adaptation Plan, and Mujib Climate Prosperity Plan lay out a need for $357.7 billion in funding (Arfanuzzaman 2024). This is significantly greater than what is possible for the Bangladesh government alone, which invests $4.7 billion in development, with about 50% of that provided by international aid (EDC 2020). Rather than focusing on the shortfall, Bangladesh has used its planning process as a key component of its effective approach to securing international finance. For example, this likely contributed to their receiving the largest disbursements from the UNFCCC Adaptation Fund in South Asia (Arfanuzzaman 2024), and one of the highest levels of low-interest credit for adaptation amongst lower-income countries (WRI 2025).
>  
> Bangladesh’s approach to planning offers elements of a blueprint to follow their success. First, Bangladesh uses advanced, internationally recognised frameworks, like CRDP, to construct integrated perspectives. They design public-private partnerships, with project selection processes and government co-funding, as part of their plans (EDC 2020). And finally, they have created a dedicated adaptation fund, the Bangladesh Climate Change Resilience Fund, to support these projects. 
> 
> While extensive evaluations of the benefits of this process are unavailable, WRI (2025) highlights the Weather and Climate Services Regional Project, which is estimated to 
> 
> As an integrated approach of conceptualisation, engagement, funding, and implementation, Bangladesh’s planning process itself is a top example of adaptation in action.
